        "title": "What is asynchronous communication?",
        "category": "Events, Effects, Forms & AJAX Fundamentals",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "Asynchronous communication means a process starts, but the program does not wait for it to finish. The program moves on to the next line of code immediately. When the background process finally finishes, it triggers a 'callback' to let the program know it's done. \nThis is vital for the web because JavaScript is single-threaded. If a network request was synchronous, the entire browser would freeze until the server replied.",
        "example": "Ordering food at a restaurant.",
        "code": "// SYNCHRONOUS (Bad): You order food, and stand at the counter frozen until it's cooked. No one else can order.\n\n// ASYNCHRONOUS (Good): You order food, get a buzzer (callback), and go sit down. You can talk and drink. \n// When the food is ready, the buzzer goes off, and you get your food.\n\nconsole.log(\"1. Request started\");\n$.get('/api/data', function(data) {\n    console.log(\"3. Data received (Callback)\");\n});\nconsole.log(\"2. This runs immediately, before data is received!\");",
