        "id": 130,
        "title": "What is request throttling?",
        "category": "Advanced AJAX, Performance, Security & Architecture",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "Throttling is a performance optimization technique that limits the rate at which a function (like an AJAX call) can be executed over time. \nIf a user triggers an event 100 times in 1 second (e.g., scrolling the page), a throttled function will only allow the AJAX request to fire once every X milliseconds, ignoring all other triggers in between.",
        "example": "Throttling infinite scroll AJAX calls to once per second.",
        "code": "// A basic throttle implementation\nfunction throttle(func, limit) {\n    let inThrottle;\n    return function() {\n        if (!inThrottle) {\n            func.apply(this, arguments);\n            inThrottle = true;\n            setTimeout(() => inThrottle = false, limit);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Only allows the AJAX check to fire once every 1000ms\n$(window).on('scroll', throttle(checkScrollPosition, 1000));",
        "tip": "Instead of writing your own throttle function, use Lodash (`_.throttle()`) in production.",
