        "category": "Advanced AJAX, Performance, Security & Architecture",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "Debouncing is a technique that delays the execution of a function until after a certain amount of time has passed since the *last* time it was triggered. \nIt is perfect for 'Search as you type' inputs. If the user types 'A..P..P..L..E', you don't want to fire 5 separate AJAX queries. Debouncing waits until they pause typing for 300ms, and then fires 1 single AJAX query.",
        "example": "Debouncing a search input.",
        "code": "let timeoutId;\n\n$('#searchBox').on('keyup', function() {\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId); // Cancel previous timer\n    \n    const query = $(this).val();\n    \n    // Set a new timer\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(function() {\n        // This only runs if the user stops typing for 500ms\n        $.get('/api/search?q=' + query);\n    }, 500);\n});",
        "tip": "Debouncing is essential for reducing server load and API billing costs in auto-complete features.",
        "mistake": "Implementing live search without debouncing, resulting in race conditions where the 'A' query finishes *after* the 'APPLE' query, showing the wrong results on screen.",
        "summary": "A technique that forces a function to wait a specified amount of time after its last invocation before executing, ideal for grouping rapid inputs (like typing) into a single AJAX request."
