        "id": 164,
        "title": "How do you ensure accessibility (a11y) when updating the DOM via AJAX?",
        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "When you update a page using AJAX (like loading new content or showing a success message), sighted users see the change instantly. However, blind users using Screen Readers are unaware that the DOM has changed because the page didn't reload. \nYou ensure accessibility by using ARIA (Accessible Rich Internet Applications) attributes, specifically `aria-live` regions.",
        "example": "Using aria-live regions.",
        "code": "<!-- HTML: A designated region for announcements -->\n<div id=\"ajax-announcer\" aria-live=\"polite\" class=\"sr-only\"></div>\n\n<script>\n$.ajax({\n    url: '/api/save',\n    success: function() {\n        // Update the visual UI\n        $('#visual-save-icon').show();\n        \n        // Update the aria-live region. \n        // The screen reader will instantly read this out loud to the user!\n        $('#ajax-announcer').text(\"Settings saved successfully.\");\n    }\n});\n</script>",
        "tip": "Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` for standard updates (it waits until the user stops typing/reading). Use `aria-live=\"assertive\"` for critical errors (it interrupts the user immediately).",
