        "title": "Explain the concept of HTTP/2 multiplexing and its impact on AJAX performance.",
        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "Under older HTTP/1.1 protocols, browsers limited concurrent AJAX requests to the same domain (usually max 6). If you fired 10 AJAX requests, 4 had to wait in a queue, causing bottlenecks. \nHTTP/2 introduced Multiplexing. It allows the browser to send an infinite number of AJAX requests simultaneously over a single, shared TCP connection.",
        "example": "The architectural impact.",
        "code": "// HTTP/1.1 Environment:\n// Firing 20 small AJAX requests simultaneously is a terrible idea. \n// It clogs the browser network queue. You should batch them on the backend.\n\n// HTTP/2 Environment:\n// Firing 20 small AJAX requests simultaneously is perfectly fine!\n// They all travel instantly down the exact same TCP pipe.",
