        "title": "What happens if an AJAX request is made while the user navigates away from the page?",
        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "If an AJAX request is in transit (pending) and the user clicks a link to leave the page (or closes the tab), the browser immediately aborts the AJAX request. \nThe `error` callback will fire (if there is time before the page unloads) with a status of `0` and a textStatus of `'abort'` or `'error'`.",
        "example": "The interrupted request.",
        "code": "$.ajax({\n    url: '/api/save-draft',\n    error: function(jqXHR) {\n        // If the user closed the tab, this might fire very briefly\n        if (jqXHR.status === 0) {\n            console.log(\"Request aborted by navigation.\");\n        }\n    }\n});",
        "tip": "If you critically need to save data (like a draft email) when the user closes the tab, you CANNOT rely on standard AJAX. You must use `navigator.sendBeacon()`.",
