        "id": 184,
        "title": "How do you configure an AJAX request to strictly accept only JSON and fail if HTML is returned?",
        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "You set `dataType: 'json'`. \nIf the server experiences an error (like a 500 Server Crash) and returns an HTML error page instead of JSON, jQuery will attempt to run `JSON.parse()` on the HTML. This will fail, triggering the `error` callback with a `parsererror` status, protecting your `success` callback from receiving corrupted data.",
        "example": "Enforcing JSON parsing.",
        "code": "$.ajax({\n    url: '/api/data',\n    dataType: 'json', // STRICT: Must be JSON\n    success: function(data) {\n        // We are 100% guaranteed 'data' is a valid JS object\n    },\n    error: function(jqXHR, textStatus, errorThrown) {\n        if (textStatus === 'parsererror') {\n            console.error(\"The server sent invalid JSON (probably HTML).\");\n        }\n    }\n});",
        "tip": "If you omit `dataType`, jQuery uses 'Intelligent Guessing' based on the server's MIME type. If the server sends JSON but accidentally uses an HTML MIME type, jQuery will NOT parse it. Always be explicit.",
