        "title": "What is the difference between synchronous AJAX and using async/await with asynchronous AJAX?",
        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "- **Synchronous AJAX (`async: false`):** Evil. Completely freezes the browser's main thread. The user cannot scroll, click, or see animations until the network replies. \n- **`async/await` with AJAX:** Perfect. The code *looks* synchronous and reads line-by-line, but the browser's main thread is completely free to run animations and accept clicks while it waits for the Promise to resolve.",
        "example": "The critical difference.",
        "code": "// BAD: Synchronous (Freezes the UI)\n$.ajax({ url: '/api', async: false });\nconsole.log(\"This logs AFTER the network finishes.\");\n\n// GOOD: Async/Await (Pauses the function, NOT the browser)\nasync function getData() {\n    await $.get('/api');\n    console.log(\"This logs AFTER the network finishes.\");\n}\ngetData();\nconsole.log(\"This logs IMMEDIATELY! The browser is responsive.\");",
        "tip": "Synchronous AJAX is actively being removed from modern web standards. Never use it.",
