        "category": "Bonus: Advanced AJAX & Edge Cases",
        "language": "javascript",
        "explanation": "HSTS is a security header sent by a server that forces the browser to ONLY communicate with that domain using secure HTTPS, never HTTP. \nIf you make a careless AJAX request to `http://api.domain.com`, the browser will intercept it and internally rewrite it to `https://api.domain.com` before it ever hits the network.",
        "example": "Preventing downgrade attacks.",
        "code": "// Server sends: Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000\n\n// Developer makes a mistake in the frontend code:\n$.get('http://api.securebank.com/data');\n\n// Because HSTS is active, the browser says:\n// \"No, I know this domain requires HTTPS.\"\n// And silently changes the request to: https://api.securebank.com/data",
        "tip": "HSTS protects your AJAX payloads from Man-In-The-Middle attacks on public Wi-Fi networks.",
        "mistake": "Relying on HSTS to fix sloppy frontend code. Always explicitly write `https://` in your AJAX URLs.",
        "summary": "HSTS is a server policy that forces the browser to automatically upgrade any insecure HTTP AJAX requests to secure HTTPS before sending them over the network."
